# Resources — distributed separately; asset files are not versioned.
# Folder stubs (.gitkeep) and INSTALLATION_GUIDE.md are tracked so the
# expected structure is visible in the repo without the actual assets.
resources/**/*.ogg
resources/**/*.wav
resources/**/*.jpg
resources/**/*.jpeg
resources/**/*.png
!resources/img/drum.png
resources/**/*.rbtheme

_external_docs/*
_future_ideas/*
_raw_assets/*
_old_stuff/*
.continue/*

# Calibration run output.
#
# corpus_scores.csv and corpus_scores_baseline.csv ARE versioned, deliberately.
# They hold only derived measurements - shortname, official rank, factor values -
# with no MIDI and no audio, the same class of metadata as any songs.dta reader
# produces. Tracking them is what makes the pilot reproducible at all: the
# reference corpus itself lives in the untracked _external_docs/ and exists on one
# machine, so if the derived table were untracked too the repo would contain no
# data whatsoever, and the coefficients that eventually ship in the helper would
# have no derivation anywhere in it.
#
# They keep earning their place after the feature ships: every remaining modelling
# question (candidate factor sets, ridge strength, per-instrument fits, grouped
# cross-validation) is answered from the CSV alone, with no REAPER and no corpus.
# What they cannot survive is a change to the factor SET - a new column means a
# full rescore - which is why exactly two are tracked (current plus the baseline a
# change is being measured against) rather than an accumulating archive.
#
# The numbered snapshots below are that archive: older factor sets (17/25/29
# columns), so they can be neither refit nor row-compared against the current one.
# The design doc tells that history better. Kept locally, not versioned.
dev/calibration/_[0-9][0-9]_*.csv
dev/calibration/_[0-9][0-9]_*.txt

# Transient protocol-run state. The report is written to a sibling .part and renamed
# onto the authority path only once it is complete, so a killed run leaves both of
# these behind - the .part is the evidence of how far it got, the .lock is what stops a
# second run writing the same target. Neither is ever committed; the finished report is.
dev/calibration/calibration_protocol_report.txt.part
dev/calibration/calibration_protocol_report.lock

*.zip